+# Official code and adaptive GPU execution
+
+Work Harness uses this policy across setup, C/D idea experiments, E execution,
+scoring, ablations, robustness, transfer and later confirmation/debug work.
+It overrides inherited per-edit approval, official-executable-only scoring and
+fixed implementation instructions in phase_autonomy. Harness sets the research
+question, evidence obligations and phase boundary; the worker owns implementation.
+
+## Install, run, inspect, adapt
+
+Start with the core paper's actual official repository and published entry points.
+Reuse the authorized GPU host, working environment, cached models/data and prior
+successful runs. Install missing dependencies there. Trace errors from real logs,
+patch versioned project copies, and run the affected workload on that host.
+Adapt dependency versions, native launchers, precision, attention kernels, batch,
+checkpointing, data loaders and output adapters to measured hardware behavior.
+No separate review/agent/approval for each ordinary repair or experiment call.
+Keep one sustained phase worker and one native experiment at a time.
+
+Initial model, model size, method and experimental settings are revisable within
+the user's research scope. Compare a few informative sizes/families in C/D when
+needed; verify actual model releases and GPU feasibility. Do not lock research to
+the first installed small model. Record whether changed precision, quantization,
+loss weighting, truncation or sampling changes the scientific comparison.
+A successful startup establishes executability. Actual scored comparisons establish
+scientific support. Neither substitutes for the other.
+
+Use ordinary code and official evaluation entry points first; avoid elaborate
+new wrappers when a small transparent adapter suffices. Preserve upstream commit,
+local patch/config, actual command, inputs, outputs and failures. Derive run identity
+from adopted runtime/context, not a hardcoded old plugin digest. Sync the complete
+current input closure, including generated controller metadata, before dispatch.
+
+## Evaluation on the actual host
+
+Prefer the official evaluator. A native launcher/dependency patch may be necessary
+on the supplied GPU. Retain benchmark tests, setup, extraction, metrics, denominator,
+timeout and error semantics; disclose deviations. An upstream Docker example or
+unavailable unshare is not the only possible launcher. Choose a supported native
+execution arrangement and verify its actual process behavior on the host. Generated
+programs need separate process execution, timeout/cleanup and appropriate access
+isolation for that operation; independent generation/training does not inherit it.
+
+If no official executable is available or usable, a community/project adapter may
+implement the published benchmark definition. Label it benchmark_adapter, with
+its actual source and revision, never official. The native verifier accepts a
+specification-backed adapter with source-to-implementation notes and live known
+correct/incorrect probes; generated-code adapters also exercise exception/timeout
+behavior. These probes qualify software, not benchmark performance. Real model
+outputs still run through the same pinned scorer and retain native metrics.
+No requirement to execute a nonexistent official counterpart. If a counterpart
+is available and parity is claimed, use faithful_harness with actual parity replay.
+
+## Keep useful work moving
+
+Missing install/config/scorer code is normally repair work owned by this worker.
+Inspect the real command, implement or adapt it, execute and read the result.
+Do not park merely because a final scorer is pending or static source inspection
+finds an interface gap. Continue independent data preparation, generation/export,
+or training that does not consume scores, while retaining unscored artifacts.
+Quality-based filtering/reward/selection must wait for its actual scorer.
+
+Use a dependency checkpoint only when no useful authorized work can proceed and
+a concrete external/runtime dependency cannot be repaired in the worker. State
+what was attempted, its real error and what external change enables recovery;
+include why independent work cannot proceed. This is contextual reasoning, not
+another per-call approval form. Ordinary GPU debug continues inside the worker.
+
+C/D permit developmental choices and iterative redesign; revisit B evidence when
+needed. E records a selected protocol before confirmatory runs. Any repair that
+changes scientific semantics creates a new protocol version and invalidates only
+affected confirmation, rather than silently merging unlike runs. Independent
+phase review checks actual evidence, strong controls, fair information/budget,
+uncertainty and failure boundaries. Technical success alone never closes a
+scientific gate. Preserve the existing goal, target, budget, history and user pause.
+
+Later-phase development stages and operational patch provenance use the supported
+[stage interfaces](cd-runtime-contracts.md#later-phase-gpu-development-and-operational-adaptations-11).
+An operational patch record permits execution and lets the phase reviewer inspect
+its actual effects; it is not another advance approval or equivalence certificate.
